{"conditionalSetIds":[],"forecasterId":"demographics-ai-2036:run1:rstar:redteam","informationSetArtifactId":"sha256:ed176b1e500945f67eb4094f80466b930f8cdbd946c460096ca55f0750d0be79","modelForecastIds":[],"modelUse":"none","prediction":{"kind":"binary","positiveOutcomeId":"yes","probability":0.34},"questionHash":"sha256:a11e957a2fbf9e72130705a8815d67b5a6c66c3444a8332ee92cdcb4248068e1","questionId":"demographics-ai-2036-cq2","questionVersion":"1.0.0","rationale":"CONJUNCT A (GDP-weighted OECD-38 core CPI/PCE avg >=2.50%/yr, 2027-2036) = 0.50. The decisive fact here was found by exactly one of three Wave-1 panelists and ignored by the other two, which is itself the finding: panelists 1 and 2 both built the OECD aggregate from US/euro/Japan blocs (P2 explicitly: 0.45xUS + 0.30xeuro + 0.09xJapan + 0.16x\"rest\") and never asked what is actually IN the published GDP-weighted OECD-38 aggregate. TURKIYE is in it, at ~4% of OECD PPP GDP, with core running 8-15%; that single inclusion adds 0.25-0.60pp to the printed number against a 2.50% threshold. P1's 0.33 and P2's 0.40 are answers to a question about an aggregate that is not the one being forecast. P2's own bloc arithmetic produced a 2.41% centre with sigma ~0.25 over a ten-year average — add 0.30pp of Turkiye and that mechanical centre is 2.71%, i.e. the threshold sits BELOW centre rather than 0.1pp above it, which flips the sign of the whole calculation. Second channel in the same direction: \"core CPI/PCE\" is left interchangeable, and US core PCE runs 25-35bp below core CPI with the US at ~37-40% of OECD-38 PPP GDP, worth 0.10-0.15pp. Third: euro-area HICP's long-delayed phased integration of owner-occupied housing plausibly lands ~2029-2031, inside the window, one-way upward.\nBUT I apply a genuine downward coupling that the panel got backwards, and it is my lens's main substantive move here. Panelists 1 and 2 both let fiscal stress raise inflation AND raise term premia at the same time. Those are substitutes at the margin, not complements: if the sovereign clears its rollover at a higher REAL yield (my CQ4 P50 of 2.45%, against their 1.75-1.95%), the adjustment has already happened in the price of duration and the inflation tax is not required. The world where OECD core averages 2.7%+ is disproportionately the world where repression is chosen and the measured real yield is LOW. So my own CQ4 forces my inflation distribution DOWN relative to a forecaster who reads 2A's fiscal block as straightforwardly inflationary. Net of Turkiye up and rate-expression down: P(A) = 0.50.\nCONJUNCT B (China headline CPI avg <1.50%/yr, 2027-2036) = 0.65. NBS headline, no back-revision, resolution-friendly; 2023-26 ran 0.2-0.7%; the Japan 1995-2015 analogue averaged ~0.0-0.3% over twenty years; 2A locks -60M population 2026-2035 and worker/retiree 4.4->2.8. The series is widely assessed as smoothed, and a lower-variance series near a 0.5-1.0% trend is mechanically likelier to stay inside a band. Against: the 2026 basket rebase (pork weight alone has historically swung headline 0.5-1.0pp); and the reflation branch Wave 1 underweighted — 2A's own pension/social-security subsidy path (2.9T RMB, 10.1% of fiscal expenditure, heading past 15%) is a transfer to high-MPC retirees, and a state that finally builds a credible household backstop releases exactly the precautionary saving holding Chinese prices down. That mechanism is INSIDE the locked 2A block rather than exogenous, and this is a ten-year window. Also RMB depreciation with food-import exposure. 0.65, below P2's 0.72.\nCORRELATION: two offsetting channels. Positive: both legs are the same OECD-bottleneck / China-glut bifurcation. Negative and mechanical: Chinese overcapacity exports goods disinflation into OECD core, and core does include core goods — but OECD core is service-heavy so the drag is weak. Net +0.05 effective. JOINT = 0.50 x 0.65 x 1.05 = 0.341 -> 34. At the top of the Wave-1 range (18/27/33) and for a reason two of three never priced: the resolving aggregate contains Turkiye.","referenceClassIds":[],"schemaVersion":"forecast-workbench.forecast/v1","sealedAt":"2026-11-04T17:31:53.488Z","seriesId":"published-panel","strongestContraryCase":"Not elicited per question. Panelist falsification signposts: {\"signpost\":\"The New York Fed's HLW r* estimate for the United States (or the Richmond Fed's Lubik-Matthes companion) is published at or below 1.0% for the 2027 and 2028 vintages, with no upward revision to the 2024-26 history.\",\"by_when\":\"Dec 31, 2028\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"The cleanest direct refutation of my central claim that the filter is revising upward into a regime break. CQ4 P50 falls from 2.45% toward 1.95-2.05% (Wave-1 territory), my variance attribution flips back to premium-dominant, CQ5 falls ~5pp to roughly 25 as the MOVE level channel weakens, and CQ6-D rises from 10 toward 15 at B's expense.\"} | {\"signpost\":\"The US 10y TIPS real yield (FRED DFII10) averages below 1.70% over any rolling twelve months, in the ABSENCE of an announced yield-curve-control, duration-shortening or captive-buyer mandate from the Federal Reserve or Treasury.\",\"by_when\":\"Dec 31, 2031\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"Mean reversion down without a repression mechanism is exactly the move Wave 1 asserted and I said had no mechanism. CQ4 P50 down ~50bp to ~1.95% and P90 down to ~3.2; CQ6-D up ~6pp and CQ6-A down ~4pp, since cheap duration signals abundant saving. If it instead happens WITH an announced mandate, my repression wedge was too small (-30bp) and only CQ4 moves, not CQ3 or CQ6-A.\"} | {\"signpost\":\"The OECD's published GDP-weighted OECD-38 core inflation aggregate for 2028-2030 prints below 2.2%/yr on the Turkiye-INCLUSIVE basis, or the OECD discontinues the Turkiye-inclusive headline aggregate in favour of the ex-Turkiye series as its reference measure.\",\"by_when\":\"Dec 31, 2031\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"Either observation kills the measurement argument that took my CQ2 to 34 (top of the Wave-1 range) and that suppressed CQ6-D. CQ2 falls to ~24, CQ6-C falls from 19 to ~14 and CQ6-D rises from 10 to ~15. This is the signpost most specific to me, because two of three Wave-1 panelists never priced Turkiye at all.\"} | {\"signpost\":\"An MSCI World peak-to-trough drawdown of >=25% occurs and G7 GDP-weighted 10y nominal sovereign yields fall by >=100bp from the equity peak date to the trough date — i.e. the bond hedge works emphatically, not marginally.\",\"by_when\":\"Dec 31, 2033\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"Confirms Wave 1's disinflationary-bust premise against my credit-event argument and resolves CQ5's conjunct B as NO for the likeliest shock in the window. CQ5 falls from 30 to ~18. It also implies abundant precautionary saving, so CQ4 P50 falls ~35bp and CQ6-D rises from 10 toward 16.\"} | {\"signpost\":\"Japan's 10y JGB nominal yield remains below 2.25% AND Germany's 10y Bund below 3.00% on a twelve-month average, with the Bank of Japan holding a policy rate at or below 1.0%.\",\"by_when\":\"Dec 31, 2032\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"Removes the Japan/Europe convergence-upward component worth ~40-50bp of my CQ4 recomposition and vindicates the low-weighted-average reading all three Wave-1 panelists reached (by a route panelist 2 described in words and then neutralized). CQ4 P50 down ~40bp to ~2.05%; CQ5 down ~4pp via the MOVE level channel; CQ3 and CQ6 essentially unchanged, since this is a price signpost, not a quantity one.\"}","updateBasis":{"kind":"launch","note":"run1 wave 2 redteam seat, effort 'rstar'."}}